00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.044
This is the last and final challenge.

00:07:42.336 --> 00:07:45.256
Prepare a 150 word description

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:52.597
of your project.

00:07:54.056 --> 00:07:57.226
Now, please wrap up all of your work
and package it for us to review.

00:07:57.435 --> 00:08:01.147
The last hour of the deadline is typically
when everything starts to crash.

00:08:01.355 --> 00:08:05.776
However, with the HP Z8 G5,
we can have multiple apps running

00:08:05.776 --> 00:08:09.197
simultaneously since it came with 128

00:08:09.197 --> 00:08:12.909
gigabytes of RAM that can even be expanded
up to two terabytes.

00:08:13.117 --> 00:08:14.368
The case is awesome.

00:08:14.368 --> 00:08:18.372
Two redundant power
supply for hot swappable M2

00:08:18.372 --> 00:08:22.126
storage, super quiet cooling system
and plenty of connectivity.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:25.755
The Z8 is the top of its line,
so it's a little bit expensive

00:08:25.755 --> 00:08:28.090
and might be a little bit of an overkill
for what you need.

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:30.426
But HP also has other versions

00:08:30.426 --> 00:08:33.846
of this workstation
which comes at a much lower price point.

00:08:34.388 --> 00:08:44.815
Check out the link in the description
if you want to know more.

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:46.609
So over the last couple of days,

00:08:46.609 --> 00:08:50.738
I've been asking our Discord community
to vote on these images.

00:08:51.322 --> 00:08:53.908
I didn't give them any background
on the video

00:08:53.908 --> 00:08:56.953
or the competition
or who made these images.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:00.498
So the vote was pretty
much based on a gut feeling.

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:06.379
But I think it's a big enough pool
to get a sense for people's preferences.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.339
So here's the results.

00:09:08.339 --> 00:09:12.343
So the winner of all three challenges
the plan challenge,

00:09:12.426 --> 00:09:17.390
the render challenge
and the text challenge was the AIs.

00:09:18.057 --> 00:09:21.227
And I think we expected the images

00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:25.481
to have a overwhelming majority
for the AIs,

00:09:25.481 --> 00:09:28.818
but we were definitely surprised
by the text challenge.

00:09:29.026 --> 00:09:32.697
You should also keep in mind that
if you look really closely at the plans

00:09:32.697 --> 00:09:36.325
that MIDJOURNEY generated,
it doesn't actually make sense.

00:09:36.659 --> 00:09:40.288
And that's because Mid Journey
is designed to produce pretty pictures.

00:09:40.496 --> 00:09:43.874
And the way that I ask people to

00:09:43.874 --> 00:09:46.627
vote is on a gut feeling.

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:51.465
So they weren't spending a lot of time
observing whether the plan was functional.

00:09:51.465 --> 00:09:55.428
This was a really interesting process
because I think we're at a point now

00:09:55.428 --> 00:10:00.016
where for certain tasks
AI can do them better than humans.

00:10:00.433 --> 00:10:02.893
So the question is, should we be scared?

00:10:03.644 --> 00:10:05.980
Is AI going to replace us?

00:10:06.689 --> 00:10:08.065
I don't want to sugarcoat things.

00:10:08.065 --> 00:10:13.195
Yes, I do think that AI will replace
a lot of jobs, including jobs

00:10:13.195 --> 00:10:18.409
in the design and architecture field,
if not now, are very near in the future.

00:10:18.868 --> 00:10:22.622
On top of what MidJourney can do,
there's a lot of other apps

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:27.043
that we didn't cover in this video,
like apps for generating massing studies

00:10:27.043 --> 00:10:32.131
or apps for Generating Plan Iterations,
all of which we do pretty manually

00:10:32.131 --> 00:10:32.715
right now.

00:10:32.715 --> 00:10:37.345
This type of AI's called
ANI artificial narrow intelligence,

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:42.725
which is the type of AI that's designed
to solve specific tasks really well.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.479
And they're only going to get
exponentially better every single year.

00:10:46.812 --> 00:10:50.274
Maybe at some point
we're also going to have AI for doing

00:10:50.274 --> 00:10:52.943
construction details or writing specs.

00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:57.198
So I think the more specific
and siloed your job is,

00:10:57.323 --> 00:11:00.993
the higher the chances are
that your job will be affected by AI.

00:11:01.118 --> 00:11:05.206
I can't really get into the details of how
to situate yourself in this transition

00:11:05.206 --> 00:11:10.294
right now, but I did make a video
on a book that does break this down.

00:11:10.628 --> 00:11:12.296
I'll leave it right here.

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:15.800
But of course,
AI can't replace architects.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:18.803
It can't take abstract constraints
and turn them

00:11:18.803 --> 00:11:22.306
into functional, conceptual
and feasible designs.

00:11:22.973 --> 00:11:26.227
It can't coordinate the work of other AIs

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.732
and it can't take liability for its work,
and it can't make clients

00:11:31.774 --> 00:11:35.152
feel safe about the millions of dollars
in the investments.

00:11:35.903 --> 00:11:38.531
There is another type of AI called

00:11:38.531 --> 00:11:42.660
general AI, or strong AI,
which tries to replicate

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:47.289
the full human cognition
and we are pretty far away from that.

00:11:47.289 --> 00:11:50.835
But once we get there,
we are pretty much screwed.

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:54.130
I think my biggest concern right now
is how A.I.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:58.217
is going to influence the next generation
of architects and designers.

00:11:58.342 --> 00:12:02.346
These tools generate
what feels like the final image,

00:12:02.805 --> 00:12:07.017
and they put out
what feels like the final answer.

00:12:07.393 --> 00:12:11.105
But it's skipping a lot of steps
to get to that stage.

00:12:11.439 --> 00:12:14.608
Architecture is not really a pure art.

00:12:15.025 --> 00:12:19.530
It's a creative response, but
it's a response to real life constraints.

00:12:20.197 --> 00:12:24.285
And these responses
come out of a very non-linear process

00:12:24.744 --> 00:12:26.704
of manipulating the form.

00:12:26.704 --> 00:12:29.165
See how that changes the function...Or

00:12:29.165 --> 00:12:32.793
maybe you can try to solve a problem
with materiality or light.

00:12:33.252 --> 00:12:36.005
It's a constant back and forth
and back and forth,

00:12:36.547 --> 00:12:39.675
all with the goal
of hopefully benefiting humanity.

00:12:39.884 --> 00:12:44.472
Even the very beginning of a project
is a very labyrinthine process,

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:49.810
and you can actually see how I'm doing it
with one of my projects right here.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:53.731
Overall, don't be afraid of
AI try to use it to your advantage

00:12:53.981 --> 00:12:57.943
and constantly keep thinking
about how you can mix your existing skills

00:12:57.943 --> 00:13:01.280
with AI to create
a whole new category for yourself.

00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:03.324
We've been having a lot of fun with A.I.

00:13:03.324 --> 00:13:04.325
in our office.

00:13:04.325 --> 00:13:05.826
We've been using CHATGPT

00:13:05.826 --> 00:13:10.247
for research, MidJourney
for generating thumbnails and storyboards.

